CAT = "uksiaIndustry"; SEG = "IND"
# Unit 1, LO1 to LO3: the industry, the SIA, legislation, arrest.
Q = [
("Which body regulates the private security industry under the Private Security Industry Act 2001?",
 "The Security Industry Authority", ["The Health and Safety Executive", "The local licensing authority", "The Home Office Licensing Unit"],
 "The Private Security Industry Act 2001 set up the Security Industry Authority (SIA) to license individuals and regulate the industry. Local licensing authorities license premises and alcohol sales, not door staff."),
("What is the main purpose of the SIA's Approved Contractor Scheme?",
 "To raise standards in security firms", ["To license individual door staff", "To set opening hours for licensed venues", "To approve venue capacity limits"],
 "The Approved Contractor Scheme is a voluntary scheme that raises performance standards in security businesses and gives customers more confidence. Individual operatives are licensed separately."),
("How long does an SIA door supervisor licence normally last?",
 "3 years", ["1 year", "5 years", "10 years"],
 "Front-line SIA licences, including door supervision, last 3 years. You then renew it, and since April 2025 a door supervisor needs refresher training first."),
("What is the minimum age to hold an SIA licence?",
 "18", ["16", "17", "21"],
 "You must be 18 or over to hold an SIA licence. The training itself is approved for learners aged 18 and over."),
("Which of these is a licensable activity under the Private Security Industry Act 2001?",
 "Key holding", ["Cloakroom work", "Ticket sales", "Bar work"],
 "Licensable sectors include door supervision, security guarding, CCTV, close protection, cash and valuables in transit, key holding and vehicle immobilisation. Cloakroom, ticket and bar work are not security activities."),
("Working as a door supervisor without the SIA licence the role needs is:",
 "A criminal offence", ["A civil wrong only", "A breach of contract only", "Allowed for 28 days"],
 "Working in a licensable role without a licence is a criminal offence under the Private Security Industry Act 2001, punishable by a fine and up to six months in prison. There is no grace period."),
("While on duty, where should a door supervisor wear their SIA licence?",
 "Where it can be clearly seen", ["In a wallet, shown on request", "Inside a jacket pocket", "Only when police ask"],
 "A licence condition is that front-line staff wear the licence where it can be seen while on duty. It lets the public and the police check who is working the door."),
("Which standard of proof applies in a civil case?",
 "Balance of probabilities", ["Beyond reasonable doubt", "Clear and convincing proof", "Proof beyond all doubt"],
 "Civil cases are decided on the balance of probabilities: which version is more likely. Criminal cases need proof beyond reasonable doubt."),
("Who usually brings a criminal case to court?",
 "The state", ["The victim", "The employer", "The venue"],
 "Criminal law exists to deter and punish, so the state brings the case and the remedy is a fine or prison. In civil law an individual brings the case and the remedy is usually compensation."),
("Which of these is usually a civil matter rather than a crime?",
 "Trespass", ["Theft", "Arson", "Assault"],
 "Simple trespass is a civil wrong. Theft, arson and assault are criminal offences that the state prosecutes."),
("Under the Equality Act 2010, which of these is a protected characteristic?",
 "Gender reassignment", ["Social background", "Football club", "Criminal record"],
 "The nine protected characteristics are age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex and sexual orientation. Social background is not one of them."),
("A venue rule looks neutral but disadvantages people of one religion without good reason. This is:",
 "Indirect discrimination", ["Direct discrimination", "Victimisation", "Positive action"],
 "Indirect discrimination is a rule applied to everyone that puts a protected group at a particular disadvantage and cannot be justified. Direct discrimination is treating someone worse because of the characteristic itself."),
("Refusing someone entry because of their race is:",
 "Direct discrimination", ["Indirect discrimination", "Positive action", "A reasonable adjustment"],
 "Treating someone less favourably because of a protected characteristic is direct discrimination under the Equality Act 2010. Entry decisions must rest on behaviour, policy and the licensing objectives."),
("Which law sets out rights such as liberty and a fair trial in UK law?",
 "Human Rights Act 1998", ["Equality Act 2010", "Licensing Act 2003", "Data Protection Act 2018"],
 "The Human Rights Act 1998 brings the European Convention rights, including liberty and a fair trial, into UK law. The Equality Act 2010 deals with discrimination."),
("Which of these is a legal requirement when a venue operates CCTV?",
 "Signs saying CCTV is in use", ["Cameras inside toilet cubicles", "Recordings kept for ten years", "Live footage shown to the public"],
 "People must be told that CCTV is operating, a named person must be responsible for it, and recordings must be handled under data protection law. Cameras must not record private spaces such as toilets."),
("Which of these is a limitation of CCTV?",
 "It cannot prevent crime alone", ["It can provide court evidence", "It can deter some offenders", "It can cut staffing costs"],
 "CCTV can deter, record and support investigations, but it cannot stop a crime on its own. It also raises privacy issues and can be damaged or misused."),
("Who may view footage from a door supervisor's body-worn camera?",
 "Only authorised personnel", ["Any member of venue staff", "Anyone who asks to see it", "Friends of the person filmed"],
 "Body-worn camera footage is personal data. It must be stored securely under data protection law and viewed only by authorised people."),
("What are assignment instructions?",
 "The duties for a specific site", ["The SIA's licence conditions", "The venue's own premises licence", "A police order to arrest"],
 "Assignment instructions set out a security operative's duties for a particular site, including emergency actions and contacts. They form part of the contract between the client and the security company."),
("What powers of arrest does a door supervisor have?",
 "The same as any other citizen", ["The same as a police officer", "Extra powers from the licence", "Powers for any offence at all"],
 "An SIA licence gives no special powers. A door supervisor can arrest only within the powers every citizen has."),
("Under section 24A of PACE 1984, a citizen's arrest can be made for which offences?",
 "Indictable offences", ["Summary offences only", "Any offence at all", "Licensing breaches only"],
 "Section 24A of the Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984 lets any person arrest for an indictable offence that is being, or has been, committed. Indictable offences are the more serious ones, such as theft, robbery and serious assault."),
("Which reason can make a citizen's arrest necessary under section 24A of PACE?",
 "To stop the person making off", ["To question them about it", "To search their home", "To recover the venue's losses"],
 "The arrest must be necessary to stop the person causing injury, suffering injury, causing loss or damage to property, or making off before a constable can take over. Questioning and searching homes are police matters."),
("Under section 24A of PACE, a citizen's arrest is lawful only if:",
 "A constable cannot reasonably make it", ["The suspect agrees to be held", "The manager has approved it", "The offence was clearly filmed on CCTV"],
 "Section 24A also requires that it is not reasonably practicable for a constable to make the arrest instead. A manager's approval or CCTV footage does not make an arrest lawful."),
("What should you tell a person when you arrest them?",
 "That they are under arrest and why", ["Only that the police are on their way", "Nothing until the police arrive", "That they must give their name"],
 "Tell the person they are under arrest, the reason, and that the police will be called. Use witnesses where possible and only reasonable, necessary force."),
("After a door supervisor arrests someone, who is responsible for that person's safety until the police arrive?",
 "The door supervisor", ["The venue owner", "The person arrested", "The licensing authority"],
 "Once you arrest someone they are your responsibility. You must keep them and yourself safe, preserve evidence and then hand them to the police."),
("Why should arrest be a last resort for a door supervisor?",
 "False arrest can lead to legal action", ["Every arrest needs approval from the SIA", "Only managers may make arrests", "The person must consent first"],
 "Taking someone's liberty is serious. A wrongful arrest can lead to civil or criminal action against the person who made it, and it puts your safety at risk."),
("When handing an arrested person to the police, what should you also do?",
 "Explain why you arrested them", ["Ask the police to sign your licence", "Release them at the door first", "Leave before the police arrive"],
 "Hand the person over, explain the reason for the arrest and tell the police about witnesses, CCTV and any property. Then record the arrest and give a statement if asked."),
("In England and Wales, which Act allows reasonable force to prevent crime or make a lawful arrest?",
 "Criminal Law Act 1967", ["Licensing Act 2003", "Equality Act 2010", "Theft Act 1968"],
 "Section 3 of the Criminal Law Act 1967 allows such force as is reasonable in the circumstances to prevent crime or to make a lawful arrest. Self-defence is also covered by the common law."),
("Which of these is a main aim of the Private Security Industry Act 2001?",
 "To remove criminals from the industry", ["To set pub opening hours", "To give security officers police powers", "To license venue managers"],
 "The Act aims to raise standards, increase public confidence and safety, and remove criminal elements from private security. It created the SIA and licensing; it gave no police powers."),
]
